T1  - Data integration and synthesis for pandemic and epidemic intelligence.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1186/s12919-025-00321-9
A1  - Tornimbene, Barbara
A1  - Leiva Rioja, Zoila Beatriz
A1  - Barral-Netto, Manoel
A1  - Castillo-Salgado, Carlos
A1  - Djordjevic, Irena
A1  - Kraemer, Moritz
A1  - McMenamin, Martina
A1  - Morgan, Oliver
Y1  - 2025//
Y2  - 20250423//
N2  - The COVID-19 pandemic highlighted substantial obstacles in real-time data generation and management needed for clinical research and epidemiological analysis. Three years after the pandemic, reflection on the difficulties of data integration offers potential to improve emergency preparedness. The fourth session of the WHO Pandemic and Epidemic Intelligence Forum sought to report the experiences of key global institutions in data integration and synthesis, with the aim of identifying solutions for effective integration. Data integration, defined as the combination of heterogeneous sources into a cohesive system, allows for combining epidemiological data with contextual elements such as socioeconomic determinants to create a more complete picture of disease patterns. The approach is critical for predicting outbreaks, determining disease burden, and evaluating interventions. The use of contextual information improves real-time intelligence and risk assessments, allowing for faster outbreak responses. This report captures the growing acknowledgment of data integration importance in boosting public health intelligence and readiness and show examples of how global institutions are strengthening initiatives to respond to this need. However, obstacles persist, including interoperability, data standardization, and ethical considerations. The success of future data integration efforts will be determined by the development of a common technical and legal framework, the promotion of global collaboration, and the protection of sensitive data. Ultimately, effective data integration can potentially transform public health intelligence and our way to successfully respond to future pandemics. Copyright © 2025. The Author(s).
