T1  - Night warming on hot days produces novel impacts on development, survival and reproduction in a small arthropod.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1111/1365-2656.12196
A1  - Zhao, Fei
A1  - Zhang, Wei
A1  - Hoffmann, Ary A
A1  - Ma, Chun-Sen
Y1  - 2014//
Y2  - 20140225//
N2  - An asymmetric increase in night-time temperatures (NTs) on hot days is one of the main features of global climate change. But the biological effects of an increased night-time temperature combined with high daytime temperature are unclear. We used six thermal regimens to simulate NTs on hot days and investigated the effects of night warming on life-history traits of the English grain aphid Sitobion avenae. Experimental temperatures fluctuated in continuous diurnal cycles, increasing from 27 degreeC to a maximum 35 degreeC and then declining to 27 degreeC gradually before further dropping to different minima (13, 16, 19, 21, 23 or 25 degreeC) representing NTs. When compared to expectations based on constant temperatures, night warming raised the optimum temperature for development by 3 degreeC, in contrast to results from experiments where temperature variability was altered symmetrically or in a parallel manner. Night warming also reduced aphid survival under heat from 75% to 37% and depressed adult performance by up to 50%. Overall, night warming exacerbated the detrimental effects of hot days on the intrinsic rate of population increase, which was predicted to drop by 30% when night-time minimum temperatures exceeded 20 degreeC. Our novel findings on development challenge the 'Kaufmann effect', suggesting this is inapplicable to night warming likely to be encountered in nature. Although many average temperature models predict increasing pest outbreaks, our results suggest that outbreaks of some species might decrease due to the effects of night warming on population dynamics. Copyright © 2013 The Authors. Journal of Animal Ecology © 2013 British Ecological Society.
