T1  - The Chronic Kidney Disease Epidemiology Collaboration (CKD-EPI) equation incorporating both cystatin C and creatinine best predicts individual risk: a cohort study in 444 patients with chronic kidney disease.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.1093/ndt/gft422
A1  - Rogacev, Kyrill S
A1  - Pickering, John W
A1  - Seiler, Sarah
A1  - Zawada, Adam M
A1  - Emrich, Insa
A1  - Fliser, Danilo
A1  - Heine, Gunnar H
Y1  - 2014//
Y2  - 20131028//
N2  - BACKGROUND: The recently introduced CKD-EPIcreat-cys equation surpassed creatinine-based equations for GFR estimation in a large cross-sectional analysis. However, its performance to predict individual risk of CKD progression and death in patients with various underlying CKD etiologies is unknown., METHODS: We recruited 444 patients with CKD GFR categories 2-4 (eGFR 15-89 mL/min/1.73 m2); baseline eGFR was estimated by the established MDRD and CKD-EPIcreat equations and by the novel CKD-EPIcreat-cys equation., RESULTS: Patients were followed for 2.7+/-1.2 years for the occurrence of the combined predefined endpoint event: death, need for renal replacement therapy or halving of eGFR. The endpoint occurred in 62 patients. Reclassification from MDRD determined categories to CKD-EPIcreat-cys categories yielded net reclassification improvements for those with the endpoint event (NRIevents) of 27.4% (95% CI: 16.7-40.0%) and for those without the event (NRInon-events) of -3.1% (-8.2 to 1.6%). Similarly, reclassification from CKD-EPIcreat categories to CKD-EPIcreat-cys categories yielded an NRIevents of 22.6% (10.2-34.3%) and NRInon-events of -11.3% (-15.9 to -6.5%). Addition of albuminuria to each eGFR equation increased the calculated risk of the outcome for a net 26-32% of those who subsequently reached the endpoint, and reduced the calculated risk in a net 21-23% in non-event patients, but only minimally., CONCLUSIONS: The CKD-EPIcreat-cys equation assigned patients who went on to have the event to more appropriate CKD risk categories than MDRD and CKD-EPIcreat, but patients without the event to less appropriate categories than CKD-EPIcreat. Addition of albuminuria marginally improved risk classification for those who had the event.
